{
  "dom_id": "step-t2246",
  "name": "Perimeters of Polygons",
  "problem": "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that ${AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m},$ and $CD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}.$ What is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?   \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14169002/assets/a74251f1c72eccad42b98e0d3fd9a7e7cd0a60018a6f03bb65ec2abc34eb1c88.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AD+AB+BC+CD \\\\  & =6+2+4+5 \\\\  & =17\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
